WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.413
BRUNO AZIZA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.413 --> 00:00:03.810
This is Bruno Aziza, and
today I am talking to Cody.

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.570
Now, Cody is going to talk to
us about real-time and anomaly

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.580
detection, one of
the design patterns

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.410
that we are delivering
to you this week.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.540
If you don't remember
what design patterns are

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.390
and our conversation with
Robert last week on product

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.090
recommendation, these design
patterns are a starting point.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.860
They're there to help you deploy
a solution for a given business

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:23.380
problem.

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.710
So anomaly detection
is a big one,

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.778
and Cody has a ton of
experience in that.

00:00:27.778 --> 00:00:29.070
I've known him for a long time.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.278
He was at Domo for seven
years, and now has been here

00:00:31.278 --> 00:00:32.970
at Google for a year.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.770
So, Cody, take us
through the genesis.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.285
Why does real-time
matter to our customers?

00:00:37.285 --> 00:00:39.660
CODY IRWIN: Real-time, I think,
matters to every company,

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.490
especially in this
digital transforming age.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:42.698
Like, everything is changing.

00:00:42.698 --> 00:00:46.910
I think 2020 has shown us very
clearly that companies that

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.450
can adapt quickly and understand
customers and understand

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.910
competitors and the
market in general

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:55.853
are those that ultimately win.

00:00:55.853 --> 00:00:57.270
And we found that
real-time really

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.353
is the solution to help
them find answers quickly.

00:00:59.353 --> 00:01:01.103
BRUNO AZIZA: So we
talked about real-time.

00:01:01.103 --> 00:01:02.670
Now, how does this
real-time problem

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.167
relate to anomaly detection?

00:01:04.167 --> 00:01:06.750
CODY IRWIN: So real-time, in and
of itself, is super exciting.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.070
Like, everyone you've
met should be like, yeah,

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.440
of course I want real-time.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.210
I want answers fast.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.620
But the reality is, if we
just do real-time, if we just

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.550
take a real-time stream
and point it at a company,

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:19.410
at a problem, it could
make things worse

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.390
unless there's some more
intelligence built into it.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:25.350
So we found that the sweet spot
tends to be combining real-time

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.230
data streams with
advanced analytics--

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.270
so AI, ML, in this
case, anomaly detection.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:34.765
So helping organizations
find, in the moment,

00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:37.140
those outliers, the unique
things that they should really

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:37.908
care about.

00:01:37.908 --> 00:01:39.200
And those could be good or bad.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.490
It could be like,
this is a risk.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.540
Worry about this.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.790
Or it could be like, hey,
something good is happening.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.540
This is different.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:46.213
Do more of this.

00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:48.630
So we found that to be really
a sweet spot with real-time.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.020
That's why [? protection ?]
is critical.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:50.910
BRUNO AZIZA: We
have to tell people

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.590
that these design
patterns originate

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.650
from customers and partners
that tell us about their need.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.960
And we go out and use the
best practices we have

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:02.100
and build this guidance, sample
code, architecture guidance,

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:05.290
so you can use those best
practices at your company.

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:08.340
So where did this story
on anomaly detection

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.220
start on your end?

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.919
CODY IRWIN: So we had a large
teleco provider approach us

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:12.730
and say, hey, I
have this problem.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.690
And originally, it was actually
more of a processing problem.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.190
They're like, we
have a lot of data.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:17.790
We have this massive network.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:21.140
We want to know what's
different in our traffic,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.160
but we can't even
process the data.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.030
So we started there.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:27.380
And the awesome thing is, with
tools like Dataflow, BigQuery,

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:29.410
we were able to meet
their scaling needs.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.110
So the requirements
were 250,000 events

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.988
per second that had
to be processed.

00:02:32.988 --> 00:02:34.530
They kind of kicked
off this journey.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.828
Like, we took that pattern,
we saw the problem.

00:02:36.828 --> 00:02:39.120
And we've been kind of like
wrapping it up in a pattern

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.078
and saying, OK, does
anyone else have this need

00:02:41.078 --> 00:02:42.390
for anomaly detection?

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:45.780
We've been amazed at how many
companies are like, yeah,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:46.753
I have a problem.

00:02:46.753 --> 00:02:47.670
I could use some help.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:49.032
It is real-time in nature.

00:02:49.032 --> 00:02:51.240
It really has turned into
kind of a rinse and repeat.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:51.810
BRUNO AZIZA: That's excellent.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.310
And that's the whole
point for why we're

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:54.610
building these design patterns.

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.870
So we want to invite you
to take advantage of them

00:02:57.870 --> 00:03:00.630
right now by clicking on the
link down below, and of course

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:03.070
suggesting more design
patterns that you

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:04.320
might need for your rendition.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.600
But before we go, I want
to talk about partners,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.730
and how they added to
the ecosystem here.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.440
One in particular,
I think, Confluent,

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.690
that you've been working with.

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:13.560
Tell us more about that.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.970
CODY IRWIN: At Google Cloud
we love, love, love partners.

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.345
And in this case, Confluent
has been a pretty key partner

00:03:18.345 --> 00:03:21.510
in helping us bridge the divide
between on-premise or other

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.093
clouds to GCP.

00:03:22.093 --> 00:03:24.093
It's like, a lot of times
people are like, yeah,

00:03:24.093 --> 00:03:25.650
I have this problem.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.600
I'd love for Google
to help me solve it.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.725
But the process for doing
the integration sometimes

00:03:29.725 --> 00:03:30.780
isn't super-clear.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.610
A lot of companies
are invested in Kafka.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.840
Confluent is basically a
managed version of Kafka,

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.590
and so they made it
very, very easy for us

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.720
to create the integration
and capitalize

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.360
on this functionality.

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:44.070
I will say as well, when it
comes to the actual anomaly

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:46.860
detection, we found that a
number of other companies

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.563
have a lot of great
proprietary models

00:03:49.563 --> 00:03:50.730
that get plugged in to this.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.910
So our architecture is
actually somewhat transferable.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:55.680
The pipeline is
pretty established.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.810
Like, we know how to do
that really, really well.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:03:59.420
We know how to integrate models.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:01.290
I'm kind of more
generic models, but we

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.020
have partners that really
can augment that and bring

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.630
specialty into the actual need.

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.120
So for some organizations,
the patterns

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.980
are like the exact solution.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:13.320
For others, the architecture
is the solution.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.240
And a model fits really
well into an architecture.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.240
BRUNO AZIZA: And so there's
value for customers,

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.160
there's value for
prospects listening to us,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.700
there's value for partners.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.250
There's value for everyone.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:25.470
Again, if you are interested
in this design pattern,

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:26.940
make sure you click on
the link down below.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.982
If you are interested in
others that we might not

00:04:28.982 --> 00:04:31.650
have built yet, don't be
shy, and suggest which ones

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.288
you'd like us to build for you.

00:04:33.288 --> 00:04:35.080
Cody, thank you so much
for the time today.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.060
I hope people are going
to reach out to you.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:38.018
We'll talk to you soon.

00:04:38.018 --> 00:04:39.890
CODY IRWIN: Thanks, Bruno.

